  "events": [],
  "notes": [
    "`VERTV` read in feet per second, from the manual and the change of altitude: the file has too few pairs of moving rows to compare, 2 of the 10 needed",
    "`HORZV` read in feet per second, from the manual and the change of position: the file has too few pairs of moving rows to compare, 0 of the 10 needed",
    "`GS Alt asl`, `TRACKER Alt asl`, `Found rocket altitude` and `GS_ASL_offset` read in feet, from the manual and `Alt AGL (ft)`: `Alt AGL (ft)` is `TRACKER Alt asl` less `GS Alt asl` and `GS_ASL_offset`, to 0.15 ft, on 3 of 3 rows",
    "`HEAD` read in degrees, from the manual and its range: it runs from 45 to 45",
    "`BATT` read in volts, from its range: it runs from 4.01 to 4.01, where one lithium cell reads up to about 4.2 V and the ground station's serial millivolts would be thousands",
    "the log has no barometer, so the record's altitude track is empty: a GPS tracker's file holds positions only, and every column is a channel",
    "the log's clock is `DATE` and `TIME`, in a zone the file doesn't state, and its zero is the first row's, \"2026-03-08 09:15:10.000\": at the first fix, `TIME` is 09:15:10.000, which read as UTC would be 2.6 h of local mean solar time at the fix's longitude",
    "the file holds one tracker's rows, \"Charlie-7\", so its channels keep the header's names",
    "3 rows have `Found rocket lat` and `Found rocket lon` both 0, as a receiver writes before it has a fix: their `Found rocket lat`, `Found rocket lon` and `Found rocket altitude` are read as gaps"
  ]
